WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:11.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  inspired by pen and paper role-playing  games  such as dungeons and dragons the rpg  video game genre  started in the mid-1970s towards the  late 70s and into the early 80s a  sub-genre began to emerge  the action-rpg arpgs still focused  heavily on storyline  quests characters stats and attributes  except rather than being turn  or menu based combat the player had  direct control over the characters in  real time  this added a new layer of challenge to  the user where coordination  and reaction time would now affect the  outcome of each encounter  [Music]  around the same time during the 1970s  another genre of video game began making  its mark in the industry  the shooter using a character  wielding one or more weapons shooters  would test the player's spatial  awareness  reflexes and reaction speed against npcs  or other player controlled characters  for more than two decades these two game  types continue to grow in popularity  games like the legend of zelda the  ultimate underworld series  doom duke nukem 3d quake  diablo goldeneye unreal tournament  we're all exploding across the industry  by the 2000s shooters and arpgs were  hitting a new peak in their stride  shooters like halo call of duty and  gears of war became household names  pushing the limits of both player versus  environment and player versus player  options  arpgs like path of exile diablo 2 and  world of warcraft  further defined the grind pushing the  limits of gameplay including character  classes  random weapons skill trees armor and  chasing the perfect stats and attributes  on all loot that could be found  it was around this time that the kids  who were playing these games in the 80s  and late 90s  entered the video game development  industry adding fresh perspective  this coupled with greater infrastructure  internet speeds and servers capable of  handling far greater numbers  and more complex battles opened up the  world to a new genre of game  that will combine the accuracy and speed  of the shooter with the character  classes  levelling and loop grind of rpgs  hell gate london is a dark fantasy arpg  first person shooter  developed by a team led by former  blizzard entertainment employees  who had previously worked on the diablo  series  set in a post-apocalyptic london in the  year 2038  it included random elements such as  weapon and armor attributes  loot drops and mob spawns  the game had single player and online  multiplayer features  however in a little over a year the  ongoing service support was shut down in  the united states and europe  it was relaunched again in 2011 2014 and  2018.  in 2007 only a month after hellgate was  released an mmo rpg  called taboola rasa was developed by  destination games  taking place in the near future it tells  the story of humanity's last stand  against an alien race called the bane  the gameplay included missions story  character creation  a dynamic battlefield where npc forces  were constantly warring over control  points and bases  and mixed elements from both shooters  and rpgs  it was released during world of  warcraft's peak and was targeted towards  fans of the title  unfortunately much like hellgate the  game never managed to find its audience  and a little over a year later would end  public service  although hellgate and tabula rasa never  found their footing  they managed to secure a path for future  additions to the genre  in 2009 gearbox software released a  first person shooter with rpg elements  called borderlands single-player and  cooperative  borderlands introduced a gritty uniquely  animated gameplay that was inspired by  various action role-playing games at the  time  such as ultima and diablo  playing through missions and side quests  players have access to random items that  can produce to 17 million different  combinations of weapons  attributes including the barrel bullets  scopes materials  and elemental damage they were also able  to access customizable vehicles  there were four character classes that  were available each with their own story  skills and ability that could be leveled  up to progress through the unique skill  trees  focus was placed on the shooter aspect  of the game first with the rpg as a  close second  this combination was well accepted by  the gaming community selling millions of  copies  and stamping the luda shooter on the map  as an upcoming genre in the industry  this led to sequels such as borderlands  2 released in 2012  and borderlands 3 2019  in 2013 digital extremes following the  success of free-to-play games  released warframe set in the distant  future  players take control of members of an  ancient race of warriors who have awoken  from cairo's sleep  they find themselves in a solar system  plagued by war with various factions  playing through mission solo or the  group of up to four players  experiences gained on mission completion  where characters can be levelled up with  better weapons and mods  these mods can be used to change  attributes and add bonuses and abilities  digital extremes free to play model can  be credited towards warframe success  where many games working in the space  have struggled to find balance with pay  to win elements  warframe avoids this by making the same  items available to be acquired  through the gameplay itself players can  choose whether to put the work in and  grind for it or to purchase items if  they want them immediately  [Music]  initially the growth of warframe was  fairly average  mediocre reviews low play accounts and  it take a little while to get off the  ground  however the developers have stuck with  the title and continue to build on the  platform  today warframe is a widely successful  title boasting around 50 million players  from the studio that created the  massively popular halo series  bungie moved on to create a shared world  ludashuda  set 700 years into the future destiny is  the story of the protectors of earth  the guardians using the power of the  light  the guardians defend the last city from  various alien threats  guardians can be one of three classes  each possessing their own set of skills  that are unlocked  as experiences gained destiny introduced  a number of open shared world areas on  the map  where players can team up and take on  different enemy races on different  planets  the gameplay is split between pvp and  pve game modes  true to their roots the pvp offers fast  pace action  similar to what was seen in halo the pve  modes  range from public events to missions  strikes and even raids for teams of six  players  initially destiny was a highly  anticipated and well-received title  but over time gained criticism for its  repetitive gameplay loop  but a series of dlcs brought the game  back on track  in 2017 destiny 2 was released  again there was a huge amount of hype  from the community but shortly after  critics and hardcore fans of the  franchise labelled the game as shallow  and many of the elements that made  disney great were stripped out to cater  for the casual player  a year later the majority of these  elements were reintroduced  inherited from their time with halo  destiny brings fine-tuned gunplay  a rich story in a distinct sci-fi  setting  bungie played to their strengths  focusing on the shooter elements first  and the rng loot and build diversity  second  this has proved to be a winning  combination as destiny and disney 2  have been upon the most successful games  in the loot of shooter genre to date  in 2016 massive entertainment dipped  their hand into the pool  and released a more realistic  military-style looter shooter  tom clancy's the division  with a darker theme than most of the  other games in the genre it is set in a  morally complicated near future  that features elite sleeper sales of  agents who are trying to maintain order  in a post-pandemic  apocalyptic new york city the majority  of the gameplay is split into two  sections  pvp and pve with the third section of  the map  where the two cross over this area is  known as the dark zone  a player versus environment versus  player area  this would lead the player to choose  what to do help fellow agents in the  fight  or turn on them stealing their loot  much like other looter shooters players  are set forth to level up their  character through missions and open  world activities  and collect weapons and armor to upgrade  their agent  the division is a third world cover  shooter with gunplay feeling a little  more realistic than other titles  however much like that of other games in  this space criticism around the endgame  and lack of gameplay loop became an  issue but also because of the heavy  focus on loot and build diversity  the developers have struggled with  balancing issues from day one  in 2019 the division 2 was released  it was praised by many calling it the  new bar that others should aim for  in the ludashuter genre however this was  quickly forgotten  as familiar complaints regarding the end  game and lack of content began to  surface  regardless both games broke sales  records upon release  and have continued to be updated with  new content over time  [Music]  in 2019 bioware stepped into the looter  shooter arena with anthem  [Music]  with past titles like star wars knights  of the old republic  mass effect and dragon age the community  had high hopes for this title  players would take on the role of a  freelancer who wear powerful exo suits  to defend humanity from the threats that  lie beyond the city walls  a third person shooter with rpg elements  anthem provides an open world experience  and different classes to level up and  specialize into  a purely pve game players can run  missions in open world activities either  solo  or with a team of up to four anthem  excelled in filling the power fantasy of  the player  through being able to take to the skies  flying through ravines waterfalls and  even underwater  weapons and special abilities feel  powerful placing the user in a position  of feeling like a superhero among  hostile forces  a huge marketing push further built the  hype around this title leading up to  release day  but as the genre is shown time and time  again anthem fell short of user  expectations  it was criticized for its grind lack of  end game and story  contributing to an experience that felt  shallow and repetitive  the game failed to meet commercial  expectations  bioware has recently confirmed that they  will be reinventing the core gameplay of  anthem  as part of a long-term plan and it will  continue to be supported  the ludas shooter genre is in its  infantile stages  as more developers explore this area of  the industry we're likely to see many  more directions and initiatives in the  space  this is only the beginning where  traditional games offer a linear path  through the story  lunar shooters are more open allowing  the player to forge their own path  further assisting the user to fully  immerse into the storytelling and rich  lore  more often than not they are always  online live service games  this obviously comes with a number of  drawbacks but it also offers an  ever-changing landscape  where players can pop in and out of  games leading to more opportunities for  random moments to occur  it means that these games often don't  really end stories  new missions map extensions are forever  being added  lunar shooters often allow the player to  get creative with the characters  both in a vanity perspective and through  their loadouts with hundreds  thousands or even millions of possible  combinations players can theory craft  and spend many hours chasing down that  last perfect piece  looter shooters require a different way  of thinking where shooters require map  knowledge  reaction speed spatial awareness looter  shooters add to this by requiring the  user to plan and strategize loadouts and  gear pieces  and encounters as found in rpgs  the time spent looking through the loot  and tweaking builds would easily match  the time spent running encounters  [Music]  however with all of these positives the  genre also seems to be plagued with a  number of recurring issues  they almost always seem to release with  development problems this leads to  months of patches and bug fixing that  hinders the speed at which new content  is released  hungry players quickly work through the  content often in days  leading to a long wait between dlcs and  updates  these weights then start to have an  effect on player numbers a lot of the  time never returning to their original  heights  another aspect of looter shooters that  could be challenging for developers  is that they often aren't competitive  enough for players who prefer shooters  yet not deep enough for rpgs  this leads to a split in the community  pvp versus pve  each wanting more content in their  preferred areas  when companies announce their product  they have the greatest division  but more often than not they are heavily  underestimating the community's ability  to eat up content  having them fall into the far too  familiar trend of lacking in-game  with a poorly polished gameplay loop  there are certainly pros and cons to the  luda shooter but it's still very much in  its early stages in the gaming industry  so over time we're likely to see changes  that will correct some of the repeated  concerns that each game has brought so  far  more and more developers seem to be  exploring the potential to take the  basic formula in new directions  in saying that titles like destiny the  division and warframe  don't seem to be going anywhere and have  solidly stamped their place in the  industry  as developers continue to balance and  then rebalance while also pushing out  new content  lunar shooters are huge offering great  depth with their constantly evolving  worlds and deep story  where looter shooters or even pc gaming  will be in the next 10 years as anyone's  guess  but there is a pretty good chance that  we'll still be organizing raids tweaking  builds  farming for that perfect piece and then  screaming about it until it gets nerfed  into oblivion  you

